Mr. Rustam bought a car for his family with a car loan from a bank. The interest rate is fixed in the contract, which means it does not change for the whole term of the loan.

The family's main car-related expenses:
• the loan payment made to the bank every month;
• fuel (methane) costs;
• maintenance: an oil and filter change every 10 000 km, brake pads, tire wear.

The family has now started driving to their dacha and to visit relatives more often than before.

You have been asked to design a new set of coins. All the coins are round and the same color, and only their diameters differ. Experts think the set must meet the following requirements:

• coin diameters must be no less than 18 mm and no more than 50 mm;
• each coin's diameter must be at least 25% larger than the previous one;
• the minting machine can only produce coins whose diameters are a whole number of millimeters (for example, 23 mm is possible, 23.4 mm is not).

Malika and Sardor’s father wants to buy a clear UV-protection coating to apply to the car windows. The store had four different liquid coatings. They wanted to find out which one blocks ultraviolet rays better.

Malika came up with the following experiment. They gathered:
• two identical clear glass plates that absorb almost no sunlight;
• a sheet of light-sensitive paper;
• pure glycerin (G) and a white titanium dioxide paste (T);
• four different coatings — they labeled them K1, K2, K3 and K4.

Glycerin lets almost all sunlight through, while titanium dioxide paste blocks it almost completely. That is why they were also included in the experiment.

Sardor put one drop of each substance into a marked circle on the first plate, placed the second plate on top and pressed it down with a heavy book. Then they put the plates on top of the light-sensitive paper and placed everything in a sunny spot. The paper is dark gray and becomes lighter the more sunlight falls on it: with a little light it turns light gray, and with a lot of light it becomes almost white.
